WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:00.870 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  a symphony orchestra can include more  than 100 musicians but one person is  tasked with bringing the music to life  the conductor  maestros are a paradox they stand at  center stage with their backs turned to  the audience  they don't make a sound but they produce  music from dozens of instruments  but what is an orchestra conductor and  where did this tradition come from and  what kind of audacity does it take to  interpret some of the greatest  masterpieces in western music  this great big story was made possible  by  be grim empowering the world  compassionately  [Music]  to get an idea of what a conductor does  we turn to gustavo dudamel the conductor  of the la philharmonic  the action of conducting is connecting  with the musicians  my right hand normally it gives the beat  the baton is an extension of the arm for  everybody to see and if it's a little  bit loud with my left hand i can say  less or i have to you know  do  an entrance to somebody or if i want  longer it's a combination of things i  think the conductor is not only the  person that  moves in front of an orchestra and shows  the tempo or the dynamics  it's a person that have to  guide and inspire  the orchestra conductor didn't become a  specialized role until about 200 years  ago but conducting goes back much  further the first recorded history of a  conductor was found on a greek tablet  dating back to 709 bc it described a  giver of time beating his stave up and  down in equal movements  since then the role has evolved quite a  bit in the 17th century the composer  jean-baptiste luli was known for banging  a large staff on the floor to keep time  in a fit of passion he hid his own foot  with the staff during a performance and  later died of gangrene  later in the baroque era composers like  johan sebastian bach were known for  conducting an orchestra while playing an  organ or harpsichord simultaneously  and the german conductor richard wagner  revolutionized the position in the  mid-1800s when he argued that the  conductor's role is to interpret the  music instead of just keeping time  every conductor have a different gesture  and have a different  connection with the orchestra that is  why  if you play the beethoven fifth symphony  every version is completely different  you have an idea you have a concept that  you have to bring to the orchestra you  have to take them in a journey through a  piece of music that is the kind of  things that make the action of  conducting or making music kind of  magical  at the end the main thing is the music  the rest of us we are elements  that bring that  alive  to be swimming  in that  unique and amazing  sea of sound and then to have the  privilege to be in front of an orchestra  and give a bit to them and they start  and they follow  not only the gesture but that beautiful  desire of  living the music  is the best thing in the world  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]

